```c
result = _gnutls_x509_decode_string(etype, tmp, slen, ret, allow_ber);
if (result < 0) {
    gnutls_assert();
    goto cleanup;
}
gnutls_free(tmp);
return 0;

cleanup:
gnutls_free(tmp);
return result;
```

If `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` fails, `tmp` is freed and the function returns an error. `ret` (which is `&auth_safe`) might have been partially modified by `_gnutls_x509_decode_string`. But `auth_safe` was initialized to `{ NULL, 0 }`, so if `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` fails without modifying `auth_safe`, it should be fine.

But what if `_gnutls_x509_decode_string` partially modifies `auth_safe` before failing? Let me look:

```c
_gnutls_x509_decode_string(unsigned int etype,
                           const uint8_t * der, size_t der_size,
                           gnutls_datum_t * output, unsigned allow_ber)
{
    ...
    ret = asn1_decode_simple_ber(etype, der, der_size, &str, &str_size, NULL);
    if (ret != ASN1_SUCCESS) {
        gnutls_assert();
        ret = _gnutls_asn2err(ret);
        return ret;
    }

    td.size = str_size;
    td.data = gnutls_malloc(str_size + 1);
    ...
    memcpy(td.data, str, str_size);
    td.data[str_size] = 0;

    ret = make_printable_string(etype, &td, output);
    ...
}
```

If `asn1_decode_simple_ber` fails, the function returns without modifying `output`. So `auth_safe` would still be `{ NULL, 0 }`.

